    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3664",
      "problem": "The graph below shows a section of the function $y=\\tan{\\left(Bx-1\\right)},$ where $B$ is a constant. What is the value of $B?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256901/assets/a20430400826e4dda99617ee02dc3669d2f414e91753eef8cfe792fd25aa49dc.png)",
      "worked_solution": "We can calculate the period $T$ by computing the difference in the $x$-values between two consecutive asymptotes. Therefore,\n\n$$\nT =  (2+\\pi) - \\left(2-\\pi\\right) = 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\tan(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the period, we get\n\n$$\n2\\pi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\dfrac 1 2.$",
